      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, where no three points are collinear, we need to use the concept of combinations. Specifically, we need to find the number of ways to choose 3 points out of the 12 points, since any three non-collinear points determine a unique plane.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12 is given by the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(9!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
