      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Each unique plane is determined by three non-collinear points. Thus, we need to determine the number of ways we can choose 3 points out of 12 points. This can be done by calculating the combination:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nApplying this formula for \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( r = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying by canceling out \\( 9! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\( 3! \\):\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nNow substitute back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6}\n\\]\n\nPerform the multiplication in the numerator:\n\n\\[\n12 \\times 11 = 132\n\\]\n\\[\n132 \\times 10 = 1320\n\\]\n\nNow divide by the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum number of distinct planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
