      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space, we need to use the combinatorial principle that the number of ways to choose 3 points (which define a plane) from 12 points is given by the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{12}{3}\\).\n\nThe formula for the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\(n = 12\\) and \\(k = 3\\). So, we need to calculate \\(\\binom{12}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(9!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(3!\\):\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication and division:\n\n\\[\n12 \\times 11 = 132\n\\]\n\\[\n132 \\times 10 = 1320\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
